One of my frustrations with Nethack is that after the beginning stages, the
different character classes lose their distinction. You become either
"Fighter" or "Magic-User".

One thing that would be nice is to make small, stealthy classes different
than large, strong classes. For instance, take the mines. There are some
open spaces in the mines, and some twisty passages.

Currently, backing into a single -file hallway or closet is a monster
fighting strategy that works for every class/race. I propose that the game
would be more interesting if this were to change.

Small races/character classes should get an advantage fighting in small
passages. Since they are smaller, they have more room to maneuver in a small
passage, and if they have small weapons, they have more freedom with that
weapon in a small passage.

Large races/character classes would have an advantage in a large room. They
have space to get a good swing in with a large weapon. In a small space,
they can't wield a large, heavy weapon effectively.

So, let's examine a 2x2 matrix of possibilities.
We have a Valkyrie or a Thief, in either a small passage, or a large room.

The Valkyrie in the small passage doesn't have much room to maneuver, so the
large weapon doesn't deal as much damage. They would also have a hard time
hitting a small creature, such as a gnome. The gnome is quicker and more
maneuverable, so it can get in a lot of hits with daggers, arrows, etc. If
the Valkyrie really gets herself stuck, a small, quick creature can kill her
while she's 'trapped' in a small passage.

The Thief in the small room is on a level playing field with the gnome.
Weapons such as daggers and arrows do not need the space to build up
momentum to be effective. But, if the thief were fighting a mumakil, that
creature is trapped like a valkyrie, and has a hard time stomping or goring
with its tusks. The Thief could likely kill a large creature if they met in
a small passage.

In a large room, the valkyrie has an advantage. She can swing her weapons
freely, and build up momentum with them, to make powerful hits. A small
creature is at a disadvantage in the large room, since a single hit can be
deadly.

The thief is that disadvantaged target in a large room. Large enemies can
move freely, making powerful, deadly hits. A thief's best bet is to stay out
of the open.

The result of these changes would be different strategies for large/small
classes. This aids in the role-playing aspect of Nethack. Thieves would
stick to small passages, and could totally avoid large creatures. Fighters
would stay out in the open where they stand a chance of making hits. A
newbie playing either role would quickly learn which strategy is
appropriate, after their Valkyrie gets knifed to death in a hallway, or
after their thief gets stomped in a room. Of course, there is not always a
safe route for each class, but that's what makes the game interesting.

If a small character class wants to dig a small passage, that should become
a lot more work (remember, they are small). Similary, clearing out a small
passage into a large room would be a lot of work, too.

